module lib/hf/ao
  The integrals of one molecule's basis functions, each computed by its
  production member and called by name: the overlap S, the core Hamiltonian
  H = T + V, and the electron repulsion integrals, moved to spherical
  functions when the molecule asks for them. The other members, and the
  sweeps that require them to agree, live in the verification layer
  (lib/hf/integrals, lib/hf/hf with `cross_check` on).

ao_c2s  @f39a86e4
  mol   the molecule

ao_in_run  @5a7e5c77
  why   Up to p shells the Cartesian and spherical functions are the same, so
        only a molecule that is spherical and has a d or f shell is moved.
  X     a one-electron matrix over Cartesian functions, `[nc, nc]`
  mol   the molecule

ao_overlap  @df6e6056
  why   S is the metric of the basis: two basis functions on different
        atoms overlap, so the orbitals solve `F C = S C e` rather than an
        ordinary eigenproblem.
  mol   the molecule: atoms, shells and primitives

ao_hcore  @59ea84f7
  why   The core Hamiltonian is the part of the Fock matrix that does not
        depend on the density: the kinetic energy of one electron and its
        attraction to the nuclei.
  mol   the molecule: atoms, shells and primitives
  T     the kinetic energy matrix over Cartesian functions, `[nc, nc]`
  V     the nuclear attraction matrix over Cartesian functions, every nucleus summed

ao_eri  @32366a8f
  why   The repulsion integrals are the costly stage: `n^4/8` distinct
        numbers. Packing them by the eight-fold symmetry of `(ij|kl)` keeps
        one copy of each.
        They come from the kernels lib/cas generated (lib/hf/mdg_eri). A
        spherical molecule's pair store holds spherical rows, so each quartet
        comes out spherical, instead of the larger Cartesian set being built
        and transformed in a second pass.
  mol   the molecule: atoms, shells and primitives
  G     the packed repulsion integrals in the run's functions: `(ij|kl)` is
        the Coulomb repulsion between the charge distributions `i j` and `k l`

  compute        compute the integrals by McMurchie-Davidson, screened; for a spherical run each quartet is moved to spherical functions as it is computed
  return the packed integrals
